WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.753
ClimateTrace is a local environmental observation integrity prototype for the IEEE ClimateChain

00:00:05.753 --> 00:00:11.506
hackathon. Community projects may exchange small logs of solar generation or rainfall before

00:00:11.506 --> 00:00:17.259
they have a shared data platform. This tool gives them a way to notice changes against a

00:00:17.259 --> 00:00:23.012
fingerprint kept separately. This is an edited walkthrough of actual browser captures with

00:00:23.012 --> 00:00:28.765
synthetic Windows narration. Every example reading is invented. No real sensor data, carbon

00:00:28.765 --> 00:00:34.518
credit, emissions reduction or environmental impact is claimed.

00:00:35.518 --> 00:00:42.090
The example has three observations: two solar generation readings and one rainfall reading.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:48.662
Each includes an identifier, an explicit UTC timestamp, site, metric, numeric value, unit and

00:00:48.662 --> 00:00:55.234
source note. The notes say these are invented examples, not sensor measurements. The prototype

00:00:55.234 --> 00:01:01.806
supports solar generation and electricity consumption in kilowatt hours, and rainfall in

00:01:01.806 --> 00:01:08.378
millimeters. Numeric values must be finite and nonnegative. The application checks real dates

00:01:08.378 --> 00:01:14.950
and matching units, but cannot tell whether a person supplied a truthful measurement.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:21.683
Sealing normalizes each reading and hashes it with its sequence number and the previous record

00:01:21.683 --> 00:01:27.416
hash. The last hash becomes the root fingerprint. The same complete normalized log produces the

00:01:27.416 --> 00:01:33.149
same root. The download contains all three records, previous pointers and hashes. An actual

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:38.882
browser download was inspected. Keep the root in a separate trusted place before sharing the

00:01:38.882 --> 00:01:44.615
bundle. The prefilled root here is only a convenience for this demonstration; it does not

00:01:44.615 --> 00:01:50.348
create independent trust or publish anything to a blockchain.

00:01:51.348 --> 00:01:56.795
The simulate button changes the first reading by one without updating its hash. Verification

00:01:56.795 --> 00:02:02.243
reports an integrity mismatch at reading one. This catches an accidental edit or a change made

00:02:02.243 --> 00:02:07.690
without rebuilding the evidence. It does not identify the person who changed it. Seven core

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:13.137
tests also cover changed previous pointers, duplicate identifiers, invalid units and dates,

00:02:13.137 --> 00:02:18.584
truncated logs and malformed evidence. These are narrow prototype checks, not a general

00:02:18.584 --> 00:02:24.032
security audit.

00:02:25.032 --> 00:02:30.792
Without an independent root, the original bundle passes its internal chain check, but the

00:02:30.792 --> 00:02:36.551
interface explicitly warns that a rebuilt chain could pass too. Someone who changes values and

00:02:36.551 --> 00:02:42.311
recomputes every hash can make another internally consistent bundle. Our tests demonstrate this

00:02:42.311 --> 00:02:48.071
limitation rather than hide it. Neither a hash chain nor a matching fingerprint proves that the

00:02:48.071 --> 00:02:53.831
observation happened, that a sensor was calibrated, or that a climate claim is valid.

00:02:54.831 --> 00:03:00.687
Here we supply a different sixty four character fingerprint. The internal records are

00:03:00.687 --> 00:03:06.543
unchanged, but verification rejects the independent root mismatch. In practice this comparison

00:03:06.543 --> 00:03:12.400
only helps if the original root came from a trusted channel and could not be replaced along

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:18.256
with the data. ClimateTrace currently has no author signatures, public anchoring, external

00:03:18.256 --> 00:03:24.112
timestamp, consensus network or access control. Those would need separate design and

00:03:24.112 --> 00:03:29.968
evaluation. There is no integrated AI model or automatic climate judgment.

00:03:30.968 --> 00:03:37.196
Adding a fourth observation invalidates the old seal and hides its download button, so an old

00:03:37.196 --> 00:03:43.423
export cannot be mistaken for the updated log. Duplicate identifiers are rejected. The verifier

00:03:43.423 --> 00:03:49.651
remains separate, so an earlier pasted bundle can still be reviewed deliberately. All

00:03:49.651 --> 00:03:55.878
processing stays in the browser and no data persists after reload. Codex assisted with code,

00:03:55.878 --> 00:04:02.105
tests, documentation and this video. This is a small data verification proof of concept, not

00:04:02.105 --> 00:04:08.333
production climate infrastructure. Next steps include trusted external root publication,

00:04:08.333 --> 00:04:14.560
authenticated authors and field evaluation; none is implemented or claimed today.
